1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,920
On November 20, 1980,

2
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:05,380
a drilling crew on Lake Peigneur had a problem.

3
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:07,860
More than a thousand feet below them,

4
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:10,500
a salt mine had a different one.

5
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:13,880
At the surface, the drill assembly was stuck at

6
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:15,820
roughly twelve hundred and twenty-eight feet.

7
00:00:16,555 --> 00:00:20,060
Underground, workers were finding water where water should never

8
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:20,620
have been.

9
00:00:21,420 --> 00:00:22,460
In a salt mine.

10
00:00:22,825 --> 00:00:25,200
Fifty-two people were below ground.

11
00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:28,660
Seven more were on the drilling platform above them.

12
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:31,500
Within hours, the platform would be gone.

13
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,060
Part of the shoreline would be gone.

14
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:37,300
And the canal connected to the lake would begin

15
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:39,000
flowing in the wrong direction.

16
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,060
Everyone would survive.

17
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:45,560
But the industrial landscape beneath Jefferson Island would

18
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:46,520
never be the same.

19
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:50,200
What happened here is often reduced to one bad

20
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:51,120
drilling mistake.

21
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:53,980
The real story is larger.

22
00:00:53,980 --> 00:00:57,740
It is about two industrial systems occupying

23
00:00:57,740 --> 00:00:59,020
the same geology

24
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:03,080
and what happened when the boundary between them disappeared.

25
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:06,320
Lake Peigneur looked simple from the surface.

26
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:08,360
A shallow Louisiana lake.

27
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:11,360
Trees and gardens around Jefferson Island.

28
00:01:12,020 --> 00:01:14,460
A drilling platform floating on the water.

29
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:16,820
But the landscape was

30
00:01:17,580 --> 00:01:17,830
three-dimensional.

31
00:01:18,590 --> 00:01:22,580
Jefferson Island rises above the coastal plain because

32
00:01:22,580 --> 00:01:25,920
of a salt dome a mass of ancient salt

33
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:28,540
pushed upward through younger sediments.

34
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:33,180
That hidden salt created industrial value long before

35
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:33,800
the disaster.

36
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:36,740
For decades, it had been mined.

37
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:41,540
By 1980, Diamond Crystal Salt Company operated

38
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:43,900
a room-and-pillar mine beneath the area.

39
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:48,100
In a room-and-pillar mine, workers remove large volumes

40
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:51,860
of material while deliberately leaving columns behind to

41
00:01:51,860 --> 00:01:52,680
support the roof.

42
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:57,180
That means the underground is not one giant empty

43
00:01:57,180 --> 00:01:57,580
cave.

44
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:00,060
It is a network of rooms,

45
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:00,820
pillars,

46
00:02:01,660 --> 00:02:03,320
passages and working levels.

47
00:02:04,420 --> 00:02:07,268
Federal records describe workings around eight hundred,

48
00:02:07,369 --> 00:02:10,800
one thousand, thirteen hundred and fifteen hundred feet

49
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:11,780
below the surface.

50
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:15,380
At the same time, the same salt structure made

51
00:02:15,380 --> 00:02:18,160
the area interesting to the petroleum industry.

52
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:21,200
Oil can accumulate around salt structures.

53
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:24,760
Texaco held an oil lease beneath Lake Peigneur.

54
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:28,740
Wilson Brothers was drilling the exploratory well from a

55
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:29,620
floating platform.

56
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:33,360
So one industry was moving horizontally through the salt

57
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:33,900
underground.

58
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:37,460
Another was drilling vertically from the lake above.

59
00:02:38,220 --> 00:02:41,700
Each could work safely only if the geometry separating

60
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:43,460
them remained understood.

61
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:46,980
And there was one physical rule that made that

62
00:02:46,980 --> 00:02:48,140
separation critical.

63
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:51,240
Fresh water dissolves salt.

64
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:54,880
That morning, the drill assembly would not come free.

65
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:58,963
Later reconstructions placed it at roughly twelve hundred and

66
00:02:59,017 --> 00:03:00,040
twenty-eight feet

67
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:04,520
close enough to the mine's thirteen-hundred-foot level to become

68
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:06,240
central to the investigation.

69
00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:08,660
Then the problem changed.

70
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:11,120
Water began entering the mine.

71
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:15,760
For the people underground, that transformed the situation immediately.

72
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:20,180
Salt mines are built to manage normal underground conditions.

73
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:22,800
They are not designed to accept a lake.

74
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:26,340
And because the surrounding structure itself was soluble,

75
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,620
no one could assume that an opening would remain

76
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:32,920
the same size once water began moving through it.

77
00:03:33,410 --> 00:03:36,940
At that point, nobody underground needed a

78
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:38,980
final answer about maps,

79
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:41,720
coordinates or legal responsibility.

80
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:44,080
They needed time.

81
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:48,160
What exactly connected the drilling operation to the mine

82
00:03:48,530 --> 00:03:51,400
would become the great argument after the disaster.

83
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:54,920
The connection itself was now the emergency.

84
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:59,655
The Mine Safety and Health Administration later recorded fifty-two

85
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:01,420
people underground.

86
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:04,780
They were not standing together beside an exit.

87
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:08,760
They were working inside a mine spread through multiple

88
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:09,980
levels and workings.

89
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:13,040
Now water was entering that system.

90
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:16,020
The mine had only one safe strategy left:

91
00:04:16,620 --> 00:04:20,040
move people out before flooding, ground failure

92
00:04:20,040 --> 00:04:23,520
or loss of access turned distance into

93
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:24,020
a trap.

94
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:27,260
Workers began moving toward the exit.

95
00:04:28,260 --> 00:04:31,000
Supervisors and crew members had to account for people

96
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,300
who had been distributed through the workings.

97
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:35,520
Some had to be found.

98
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:38,640
Some had to travel through a mine that was

99
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:42,140
changing from a workplace into an emergency scene.

100
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:46,820
The official investigation later recognized several employees

101
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:50,040
for leadership and search actions during the evacuation.

102
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:55,120
That matters because successful evacuations can look inevitable

103
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:56,200
after the fact.

104
00:04:56,820 --> 00:04:57,440
They are not.

105
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:00,960
The workers did not know how large the connection

106
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:01,660
would become.

107
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:04,580
They did not know how quickly the inflow would

108
00:05:04,580 --> 00:05:04,920
grow.

109
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:08,420
They only knew that water was entering a salt

110
00:05:08,420 --> 00:05:09,960
mine beneath a lake.

111
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:12,840
And less than an hour after the inflow was

112
00:05:12,840 --> 00:05:15,720
discovered, all fifty-two people were out.

113
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:19,400
No one underground was injured in the inundation.

114
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:23,340
The spectacle of Lake Peigneur can make that fact

115
00:05:23,340 --> 00:05:25,420
feel secondary. It is not.

116
00:05:26,120 --> 00:05:28,300
Before the lake became a national story,

117
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:31,705
this was a mine emergency with more than fifty

118
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:32,960
people below ground.

119
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:35,380
And the emergency plan worked.

120
00:05:36,420 --> 00:05:40,020
Above them, the seven-man drilling crew also got clear.

121
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:43,380
Then the drilling platform disappeared.

122
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:47,020
That was the point where the failure stopped looking

123
00:05:47,020 --> 00:05:50,660
like a problem inside an industrial system and

124
00:05:50,660 --> 00:05:53,960
started looking like the landscape itself was moving.

125
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:57,560
The lake began pulling toward the failure zone.

126
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:02,480
Floating equipment, boats, trees and shoreline material

127
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:03,740
moved with it.

128
00:06:04,420 --> 00:06:07,440
The opening at the surface expanded as the ground

129
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:08,540
beneath it failed.

130
00:06:09,540 --> 00:06:12,380
Water was not simply draining from a stable bowl.

131
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:14,900
The bowl was changing.

132
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:18,700
The edge of the lake was collapsing into the

133
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:19,180
process.

134
00:06:20,065 --> 00:06:20,315
Post-disaster

135
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:24,640
geological work put the permanent land loss at

136
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:26,420
roughly sixty-eight acres.

137
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:30,480
That number is easier to understand if you imagine

138
00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:33,500
the shoreline as part of the machinery of the

139
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:34,000
accident.

140
00:06:34,940 --> 00:06:38,000
Every section of ground lost around the opening changed

141
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:39,280
the shape of the basin.

142
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:43,340
Every change in shape gave the moving water a

143
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:43,980
new path.

144
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:48,000
What had begun as a drilling emergency was now

145
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,740
reshaping Jefferson Island.

146
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:54,280
A normal pipe stays roughly the same size while

147
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:55,860
water passes through it.

148
00:06:56,400 --> 00:06:59,060
A failure through salt does not have to.

149
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:02,100
Fresh water contacts the salt surface.

150
00:07:03,180 --> 00:07:05,000
Salt dissolves into the flow.

151
00:07:05,940 --> 00:07:07,640
A narrow path can enlarge.

152
00:07:08,920 --> 00:07:10,400
Loss of salt can weaken the roof,

153
00:07:10,880 --> 00:07:12,680
pillars or surrounding ground.

154
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:15,720
That creates more space for water.

155
00:07:16,460 --> 00:07:18,840
More water exposes more salt.

156
00:07:19,620 --> 00:07:21,120
And the cycle can accelerate.

157
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:26,540
Flow. Dissolution. Enlargement and failure.

158
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:28,040
More flow.

159
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:31,060
The crucial idea is feedback.

160
00:07:32,180 --> 00:07:34,920
The failure was not merely carrying water through a

161
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:35,760
fixed opening.

162
00:07:36,580 --> 00:07:39,560
The water could help enlarge the system that was

163
00:07:39,560 --> 00:07:40,520
carrying the water.

164
00:07:41,280 --> 00:07:44,860
That is why the phrase "one small hole drained

165
00:07:44,860 --> 00:07:47,800
the lake" misses the engineering story.

166
00:07:48,740 --> 00:07:51,100
Whatever the initiating pathway looked like,

167
00:07:51,460 --> 00:07:53,620
it did not have to stay that size.

168
00:07:54,420 --> 00:07:57,700
The accident was modifying the system while the system

169
00:07:57,700 --> 00:07:58,340
was failing.

170
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:01,440
Once enough water was moving underground,

171
00:08:02,140 --> 00:08:03,680
gravity supplied the energy.

172
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:07,600
The lake itself became part of the failure mechanism.

173
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:10,620
Water rushed toward the opening.

174
00:08:11,780 --> 00:08:12,920
Ground continued to collapse.

175
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:17,320
The receiving space was not a concrete reservoir

176
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:18,640
with fixed walls.

177
00:08:19,340 --> 00:08:22,560
It was a mine carved through a soluble salt

178
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:26,200
structure. The famous whirlpool was only

179
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:27,380
the surface expression.

180
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:31,980
Below it, water was entering an industrial void built

181
00:08:31,980 --> 00:08:33,680
across multiple levels.

182
00:08:34,460 --> 00:08:37,920
Above it, the lake was losing both water and

183
00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:38,540
shoreline.

184
00:08:39,420 --> 00:08:41,420
Those two systems were now coupled.

185
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:44,120
The faster the lake fed the failure,

186
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:47,500
the more the failure could change the route available

187
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:48,380
to the lake.

188
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,580
The relationship between water, mine, rock and

189
00:08:52,580 --> 00:08:55,940
air was being rewritten in real time.

190
00:08:56,840 --> 00:08:58,180
And as the lake level dropped,

191
00:08:58,700 --> 00:09:01,940
the consequences reached beyond Jefferson Island.

192
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:05,800
They changed the direction of another body of water.

193
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:10,380
Lake Peigneur connected southward through the Delcambre Canal

194
00:09:10,380 --> 00:09:12,220
toward Vermilion Bay.

195
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:16,820
Under normal conditions, that connection carried water away

196
00:09:16,820 --> 00:09:17,480
from the lake.

197
00:09:18,200 --> 00:09:21,300
Now the lake had fallen so far that the

198
00:09:21,300 --> 00:09:22,980
hydraulic relationship reversed.

199
00:09:23,680 --> 00:09:26,040
The lake became the lower destination.

200
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:28,860
The canal began feeding it.

201
00:09:28,860 --> 00:09:32,700
Water from the Gulf side moved inland toward

202
00:09:32,700 --> 00:09:33,800
the emptied basin.

203
00:09:35,120 --> 00:09:38,540
Historical Louisiana accounts describe what happened next as

204
00:09:38,540 --> 00:09:42,400
a temporary waterfall roughly one hundred and fifty feet

205
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:42,880
high.

206
00:09:43,780 --> 00:09:46,080
For a place as flat as coastal Louisiana,

207
00:09:46,540 --> 00:09:48,480
the image is almost absurd.

208
00:09:49,160 --> 00:09:51,240
A canal flowing the wrong way.

209
00:09:51,980 --> 00:09:53,360
Salt water moving inland.

210
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:57,740
A temporary waterfall appearing where there had been no

211
00:09:57,740 --> 00:09:58,040
cliff.

212
00:09:58,860 --> 00:10:01,300
But the water was still obeying gravity.

213
00:10:02,180 --> 00:10:04,420
The landscape had changed underneath it.

214
00:10:04,860 --> 00:10:06,680
The canal had not moved on the map.

215
00:10:07,480 --> 00:10:10,300
The lake had changed its elevation enough to reverse

216
00:10:10,300 --> 00:10:11,020
the system.

217
00:10:11,820 --> 00:10:13,780
When the major physical sequence ended,

218
00:10:14,260 --> 00:10:15,740
the mine was inundated.

219
00:10:16,420 --> 00:10:18,020
The drilling platform was gone.

220
00:10:18,720 --> 00:10:20,340
The shoreline had been cut back.

221
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:24,000
The lake had been transformed from the shallow basin

222
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,340
that existed before the accident into a much deeper

223
00:10:27,340 --> 00:10:31,060
one. The industrial system beneath it was finished.

224
00:10:31,780 --> 00:10:34,680
But the most important number is not the final

225
00:10:34,680 --> 00:10:36,820
depth. It is zero.

226
00:10:37,660 --> 00:10:38,600
No human deaths.

227
00:10:39,280 --> 00:10:42,640
All fifty-two people recorded underground escaped.

228
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:45,820
The seven drilling workers above them got clear.

229
00:10:46,520 --> 00:10:48,340
That does not make the event small.

230
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,220
It makes the evacuation extraordinary.

231
00:10:51,940 --> 00:10:54,940
An accident violent enough to erase part of a

232
00:10:54,940 --> 00:10:58,360
shoreline, destroy a working mine and reverse a

233
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:01,500
canal ended without a human fatality.

234
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:04,980
By now, the natural question seems obvious.

235
00:11:05,860 --> 00:11:07,960
Who put the drill in the wrong place?

236
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:11,240
That is where the popular version of Lake Peigneur

237
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:13,600
often becomes cleaner than the evidence.

238
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:18,380
A widely repeated story says a coordinate or surveying

239
00:11:18,380 --> 00:11:21,060
error sent the well into the mine.

240
00:11:21,700 --> 00:11:25,240
The official federal investigation did not close the case

241
00:11:25,240 --> 00:11:26,040
that neatly.

242
00:11:26,900 --> 00:11:30,340
After the inundation, the relevant mine workings could not

243
00:11:30,340 --> 00:11:32,280
simply be entered and inspected.

244
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:36,280
The accident had destroyed access to the best evidence.

245
00:11:37,180 --> 00:11:39,340
Critical physical information was underwater,

246
00:11:40,140 --> 00:11:41,440
displaced or gone.

247
00:11:42,360 --> 00:11:46,160
Federal mine-safety investigators reconstructed an estimated

248
00:11:46,385 --> 00:11:50,220
drill-hole location near the mine's thirteen-hundred-foot level.

249
00:11:50,780 --> 00:11:54,560
Diamond Crystal argued that the drilling operation had penetrated

250
00:11:54,560 --> 00:11:57,400
the roof of an excavated mine room.

251
00:11:58,220 --> 00:12:00,900
Other experts disputed that reconstruction.

252
00:12:01,960 --> 00:12:04,500
More than two years later, during the settlement process,

253
00:12:05,040 --> 00:12:08,040
experts were still disagreeing over whether the well had

254
00:12:08,040 --> 00:12:11,200
entered the mined room in the way Diamond Crystal

255
00:12:11,200 --> 00:12:13,360
alleged. That distinction matters.

256
00:12:14,220 --> 00:12:17,040
Investigators could document the drilling operation.

257
00:12:17,740 --> 00:12:18,960
They could document the mine.

258
00:12:19,580 --> 00:12:21,360
They could document the inundation.

259
00:12:22,260 --> 00:12:24,820
They could estimate where the well may have intersected

260
00:12:24,820 --> 00:12:25,900
the underground system.

261
00:12:26,800 --> 00:12:30,400
But the disaster had made the decisive physical scene

262
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:31,420
inaccessible.

263
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:34,460
So two statements can both be true.

264
00:12:35,080 --> 00:12:38,720
The drilling operation and mine became hydraulically connected.

265
00:12:39,420 --> 00:12:42,920
And the precise initiating technical error could not later

266
00:12:42,920 --> 00:12:44,500
be established with certainty.

267
00:12:44,980 --> 00:12:46,820
That is not a weakness in the story.

268
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:49,880
It is the reason evidence matters.

269
00:12:51,080 --> 00:12:53,100
The uncertainty did not prevent lawsuits.

270
00:12:54,340 --> 00:12:57,468
Diamond Crystal originally sued Texaco for two hundred and

271
00:12:57,516 --> 00:12:58,955
sixty million dollars.

272
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:00,540
In 1983,

273
00:13:01,320 --> 00:13:05,200
Diamond Crystal accepted a thirty-two-million-dollar settlement from

274
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:06,880
Texaco and Wilson Brothers.

275
00:13:07,980 --> 00:13:11,561
Live Oak Gardens interests received twelve-point-eight million dollars

276
00:13:11,680 --> 00:13:14,560
in a separate settlement involving the companies.

277
00:13:15,370 --> 00:13:17,440
Those numbers resolved claims.

278
00:13:18,240 --> 00:13:20,360
They did not restore the underground system.

279
00:13:21,040 --> 00:13:22,840
They did not reopen the mine.

280
00:13:23,820 --> 00:13:25,980
And they did not turn the settlement into a

